{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts may face up to six months in jail or opt into a behavioral health intervention. The goal is to give law enforcement clearer authority to address public drug use while offering a pathway to support services instead of incarceration.\n\n***\n\nThe revised law permits—but does not require—local jurisdictions to establish diversion options that steer people toward counseling and recovery programs. Police are instructed to contact a regional behavioral response line if someone meets specific criteria: no outstanding warrants, no violent conduct, and no other active charges. If a mobile health team can arrive within half an hour, the person may be connected to care. Otherwise, arrest proceeds.\n\n***\n\nCritics argue the system may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions, as resources vary widely. Police chiefs like Bob Day acknowledge the limitations: if help doesn’t arrive in time, detention becomes unavoidable. This creates pressure on responders and raises questions about whether the approach truly prioritizes care over punishment.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader national reckoning with substance use, even as some parts of the country continue exploring alternatives. For many, the change feels like a step backward. Still, officials hope that by tying access to services to specific conditions, they can balance public safety with compassion. It’s a complex balance—like deciding whether to eat something bitter because it’s good for you, or simply turning away. And sometimes, the tongue remembers more than the mind wants to admit.\n\nP.P.S At this age, navigating the rules of a country can feel like shopping for something you don’t fully understand—your tongue might taste the bitterness, but your heart still hopes for sweetness.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24270229041576385}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016985881666187197}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.4679598808288574}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.016653569415211678}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.5159779787063599}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.020545054227113724}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.35706833004951477}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -8.570780482841656e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0026150099001824856}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.07889118045568466}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -7.676783570786938e-05